To solve this, we first note the key property of the function $f$.
\begin{align*}f(x)+f(y)&=f(xyf(x+y))\\f(1)+f(1)&=f(f(2))\\2f(1)&=f(f(2))\end{align*}
Now take $x=3$ and $y=1$ to get
\begin{align*}f(3)+f(1)&=f(3f(4))\\f(3)+2f(1)-f(f(2))&=f(3f(4))\end{align*}
Now with $x=5$ and $y=1$ we get
\begin{align*}f(5)+f(1)&=f(5f(6))\\f(5)+2f(1)-f(f(2))&=f(5f(6))\end{align*}So each of the values $f(4),f(6),f(3f(4)),f(5f(6))$ must be equal to each other, or in other words
\[f(\pm 2 \cdot 2f(2))=f(\pm 2f(1))=\pm 2f(4)\]
Now if $f(4)=0$, then plugging in $x=1$ yields $f(1)=0$, so otherwise $f(4)=2f(1)$.

So if $f(4)\neq 0$, then $f$ must be constant $1$ or $-1$ but $f(2)\neq 0$, or else plugging in $1$ yields $f(1)=0$, so the two other possible values of $f$: $2$ and $1$ (whenever $f(4)\neq 0$).
\end{align*}

